{"task_id": "Mbpp/232", "solution": "def larg_nnum(input_list: list, n: int) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Takes a list and an integer n and returns a list containing the n largest items from the list.\"\"\"\n    # Helper function for input validation.\n    def _validate_inputs(input_list: list, n: int) -> None:\n        if not isinstance(n, int) or n < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n        if n > len(input_list):\n            raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' cannot exceed the number of items in the list.\")\n\n    # Helper function for edge case handling.\n    def _handle_edge_cases(input_list: list, n: int) -> list | None:\n        if not input_list or n == 0:\n            return []\n        return None\n\n    # Handle edge cases first.\n    edge_case_result = _handle_edge_cases(input_list, n)\n    if edge_case_result is not None:\n        return edge_case_result\n\n    # Validate inputs.\n    _validate_inputs(input_list, n)\n\n    # Core logic: Sort the list in descending order and take the first n items.\n    sorted_list = sorted(input_list, reverse=True)\n    return sorted_list[:n]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/237", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple, Any, Dict\ndef check_occurences(input_list: List[Tuple[Any, ...]]) -> Dict[Tuple[Any, ...], int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Canonicalizes each tuple in the input list by sorting its elements,\n    then counts the occurrences of each unique canonicalized tuple.\n    \"\"\"\n    occurrence_map: Dict[Tuple[Any, ...], int] = {}\n    for original_tuple in input_list:\n        canonical_tuple = tuple(sorted(list(original_tuple)))\n        occurrence_map[canonical_tuple] = occurrence_map.get(canonical_tuple, 0) + 1\n    return occurrence_map"}
