{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of possible sequences of length n,\n    where elements are positive, <= m, and >= twice the previous.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # This is a private helper function for the recursive counting logic.\n    def _count_sequences_recursive(current_length: int, current_last_element: int) -> int:\n        # Base case: if the sequence has reached the desired length n, we found one valid sequence.\n        if current_length == n:\n            return 1\n\n        # Initialize count for sequences extending from the current element.\n        count = 0\n        # Iterate through possible values for the next element in the sequence.\n        # The next element must be at least twice the current_last_element\n        # and not exceed m.\n        for next_val in range(2 * current_last_element, m + 1):\n            count += _count_sequences_recursive(current_length + 1, next_val)\n        return count\n\n    total_count = 0\n    # Start the sequences with every possible first element from 1 to m.\n    # Each first element must be a positive integer and less than or equal to m.\n    for first_val in range(1, m + 1):\n        total_count += _count_sequences_recursive(1, first_val)\n    return total_count"}
