{"task_id": "Mbpp/462", "solution": "def combinations_list(input_list: list) -> list[list]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates all possible unique combinations of elements from the input list,\n    including the empty list, following a specific order.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = [[]]\n    for item in input_list:\n        # For each item, create new combinations by appending it to existing ones.\n        new_combinations = []\n        for existing_combination in result:\n            new_combinations.append(existing_combination + [item])\n        result.extend(new_combinations)\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product formed by multiplying numbers of an increasing subsequence.\n    An increasing subsequence must have at least two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    dp = [num for num in arr]  # dp[i] stores the max product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i\n    \n    # We need to keep track of a second array to indicate if dp[i] is a product of at least two elements\n    # Or initialize dp[i] with arr[i] and use a flag or check if dp[i] > arr[i] (which isn't strictly true for all cases)\n    # A cleaner way is to track actual max_product_so_far that is guaranteed to be from length >= 2\n    \n    overall_max_product = 0\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                # If arr[i] can extend the increasing subsequence ending at arr[j]\n                # Then dp[i] could be dp[j] * arr[i]\n                \n                # IMPORTANT: Only update dp[i] if arr[j] is not 0 to avoid incorrect zero products\n                # and to ensure dp[j] represents a valid product.\n                # However, the requirement does not specify positive numbers only.\n                # Let's assume numbers can be positive, negative or zero.\n                \n                # If dp[j] was just arr[j] (meaning a subsequence of length 1 ending at j)\n                # then dp[j] * arr[i] forms a subsequence of length 2\n                \n                # If dp[j] was already a product of length >= 2, then dp[j] * arr[i] forms length >= 3\n                \n                # The issue is distinguishing between a DP value that's just arr[i] (length 1)\n                # and one that's an actual product (length >= 2).\n                \n                # Let's adjust dp[i] to store the max product of an increasing subsequence of length >= 1 ending at i.\n                # And overall_max_product stores the max product of length >= 2.\n                \n                # If arr[j] itself is 0, dp[j] could be 0. Product with 0 is 0.\n                # This could cause issues if non-zero products are possible.\n                # The question implies positive increasing subsequences for products.\n                # Example: [3, 100, 4, 5, 150, 6] -> all positive.\n\n                # Let's follow the standard DP approach for LIS sum/product.\n                # dp[i] = max product of an increasing subsequence ending with arr[i] (length >= 1)\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] * arr[i])\n        \n        # After calculating dp[i], if dp[i] is greater than arr[i], it implies that\n        # arr[i] was combined with at least one previous element (arr[j]) that satisfied arr[i] > arr[j].\n        # In this case, dp[i] represents a product of a subsequence of length at least 2.\n        # So we can update overall_max_product.\n\n        # What if arr[i] is 0? If arr[j] is positive and dp[j] is positive, then dp[j] * 0 = 0.\n        # This could wrongly make dp[i] = 0, even if arr[i] could extend to a larger product.\n        # But this is specific to 0. The design context example only has positive numbers.\n        # Assuming only positive numbers for now based on the example and problem type.\n\n        # If dp[i] > arr[i], it implies arr[i] was multiplied by a previous term, forming a subsequence of length >= 2.\n        # This condition is crucial for ensuring we only consider products of subsequences with at least two elements.\n        if dp[i] > arr[i]:\n            overall_max_product = max(overall_max_product, dp[i])\n            \n    return overall_max_product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/471", "solution": "def find_remainder(arr: list[int], n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the product of all elements in the input list `arr` modulo `n`.\n    The modulo operation is applied at each step to manage intermediate product size.\n    \"\"\"\n    product = 1\n    for element in arr:\n        product = (product * element) % n\n    return product"}
