{"task_id": "Mbpp/559", "solution": "import math\nfrom typing import List\ndef max_sub_array_sum(numbers: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of the largest contiguous sublist within a given list of integers\n    using Kadane's algorithm principles.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_so_far = -math.inf  # Stores the maximum sum found across all sublists.\n    current_max = 0         # Stores the sum of the current contiguous sublist.\n\n    for number in numbers:\n        current_max += number\n        if current_max > max_so_far:\n            max_so_far = current_max\n        if current_max < 0:\n            current_max = 0\n            \n    # Handle the case where all numbers are negative, and max_so_far was only updated\n    # when current_max was also negative and larger than previous max_so_far.\n    # If the list consists of only negative numbers, max_so_far might remain -inf\n    # if all `current_max` values were less than 0 and reset, and max_so_far was never updated past -inf.\n    # However, the current logic correctly handles this: for [-5, -2, -8], max_so_far will be -2.\n    # The initial -math.inf handles the edge cases correctly by ensuring any number from the list\n    # will be greater than -math.inf, and thus max_so_far will be updated with at least the largest single element.\n    # A more robust check for an empty list or if all numbers are negative and the desired output\n    # is the largest single number would ensure max_so_far is never 0 if all numbers are negative.\n    # The current initialization and logic correctly derive the largest single negative number if all inputs are negative.\n    # Example: numbers = [-2, -3, -1] -> max_so_far = -1\n    if max_so_far == -math.inf and numbers:\n        return max(numbers)\n    elif not numbers:\n        return 0 # Or raise an error, depending on desired behavior for empty list. For simplicity, returning 0.\n    \n    return max_so_far"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/564", "solution": "def count_Pairs(numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of possible unordered pairs where both elements are unequal.\n    Iterates through all unique pairs of indices (i, j) with i < j and increments a counter if numbers[i] != numbers[j].\n    \"\"\"\n    count = 0\n    n = len(numbers)\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if numbers[i] != numbers[j]:\n                count += 1\n    return count"}
